The Apache-2.0 text, which applies to the parts that follow the project below, is in licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.

EasyLiDAR was written from scratch. No source code of the programs below is included in it.
They are credited because EasyLiDAR follows their published methods or documented behaviour,
or because its structure builds on a sister project.

- RustGIS (Apache-2.0): the LAS reader's approach, the layered architecture and the look of the
  interface.
- FUSION / LDV, R. J. McGaughey, USDA Forest Service (public domain): behaviour of GroundFilter,
  CanopyModel, GridMetrics, ClipData and PolyClipData, reproduced from its manual and by testing.
- MCC-LIDAR, Green Code LLC (Apache-2.0): the multiscale curvature classification of Evans and
  Hudak (2007) and its documented parameters.
- CSF, Jianbo Qi (Apache-2.0): the cloth simulation filter of Zhang et al. (2016).
- lidR, J.-R. Roussel et al. (GPL-3): only its documented behaviour was observed, by running it as a
  separate program; no code was used. Tree segmentation follows Popescu and Wynne (2004), Dalponte
  and Coomes (2016) and Silva et al. (2016).
- OxiGeo, COOLJAPAN OU (Apache-2.0): reference for which terrain products are worth having (slope,
  aspect, hillshade, curvatures, drainage, viewshed). Its source code is not included; the terrain
  analysis follows the published methods of Horn (1981), Zevenbergen and Thorne (1987), Wilson et
  al. (2007), Weiss (2001), O'Callaghan and Mark (1984), Beven and Kirkby (1979) and Barnes,
  Lehman and Mulla (2014).
- GDAL / gdaldem (MIT): the conventions of its slope, aspect, hillshade, roughness, TRI and TPI
  (windows over missing neighbours, units), checked against an independent implementation.
- WhiteboxTools, John Lindsay (MIT): inspiration for the choice of utilities (thinning, point
  statistics, tiling, contours).
- Third-party libraries: see THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md.

The names FUSION, LAStools, lidR, Global Mapper, WhiteboxTools and FugroViewer belong to their
owners and appear only to say what a feature is comparable to.
